    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. can you dive a bit deeper into the actual signaling mechanism? specifically how the amino acid sensing is localized to the lysosome and what role the Rag GTPases play in that mTOR activation process? i want to be able to explain the exact chain of events to the client.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Leucine is sensed specifically by the Rag GTPase complex anchored at the lysosomal membrane, which functions as a critical cellular docking station for mTORC1. When leucine levels are sufficient, Rags undergo a GTP binding change that recruits mTORC1 to the lysosomal surface, preventing its cytosolic inhibition by GATOR1 while allowing GLUD1 enzyme activation. This localization triggers mTORC1 to phosphorylate key kinases like S6K1 and 4E-BP1, initiating the rapid translation of mRNAs for muscle proteins. You can explain this to your client as a precise \"chassis alignment\" event where the lysosome acts as the command center, ensuring that essential nutrients are consumed only when the anabolic machinery is properly positioned to build muscle.",
      "source": "target",
